.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH SIN 3M
.SH NAME
sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2 \- trigonometric functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double sin(double x);
.PP
.B double cos(double x);
.PP
.B double tan(double x);
.PP
.B double asin(double x);
.PP
.B double acos(double x);
.PP
.B double atan(double x);
.PP
.B double atan2(double y, double x);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sin, cos
and
.I tan
return trigonometric functions of radian arguments.
The magnitude of the argument should be checked
by the caller to make sure the result is meaningful.
.PP
.I Asin
returns the arc sin in the range \-\(*p/2 to \(*p/2.
.PP
.I Acos
returns the arc cosine in the range
0 to \(*p.
.PP
.I Atan
returns the arc tangent of
.I x
in the range \-\(*p/2 to \(*p/2.
.PP
.I Atan2
returns the arc tangent of
.I y/x
in the range \-\(*p to \(*p.
.B Note:
v7's page named the arguments the other way round and called the result the arc
tangent of
.IR x/y .
The routine is the same one; the names here are C11's, and <math.h> declares it
.BR "atan2(double y, double x)" .
.SH SEE ALSO
exp(3), sinh(3), intro(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Arguments of magnitude greater than 1
cause
.I asin
and
.I acos
to return value 0;
.I errno
is set to EDOM.
The value of
.I tan
at its singular points is HUGE_VAL with the sign of the pole, and
.I errno
is set to ERANGE.
.B Note:
that is the "would have been an infinity" case, for which this machine has no
value; HUGE_VAL is the largest finite one.
.PP
Nothing on this page can overflow.
.I Atan
and
.I atan2
evaluate their fit on an argument reduced to [\-0.414, 0.414], and
.I asin
and
.I acos
reduce to
.I atan
after their range check, so no error return but the two above exists.
.SH BUGS
The value of
.I tan
for arguments greater than about 2**40 is garbage.
.B Note:
v7 said 2**31, which was its own mantissa and integer widths speaking.  Here the
reduction carries the integer part of x/\(*p through an
.BR int ,
which holds 41 bits, and above 2**40 a
.B double
has no fractional bits left in any case.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The reduction crossover was a width, not a bound
v7's
.I sin
reads
.BR "if (x > 32764)" ,
and that number is not mathematical: it is the last multiple of the quarter-turn
below 2**15, past which the PDP-11's
.B int
overflowed, so above it v7 fell back on a slower reduction through
.IR modf (3).
.PP
Here
.B int
is 41 bits, and the real point past which the fast integer reduction loses bits
is 2**40 \- the width of the mantissa, and the same constant
.I modf
itself keys on.  The crossover moves out to 2**40 accordingly, so the fast path
serves the whole range a program is likely to ask for.  A magnitude past 2**40
has no fractional turn left to speak of anyway.
.SS atan needed one line of C11
v7's first line of
.I atan.c
reads
.BR "double static sq2p1" ,
which this front end rejects: the storage class has to precede the type.  The
routine is otherwise unchanged, the closing
.B if ((y + x) == y)
test of
.I atan2
included \- that is v7's way of asking whether the angle is a right angle, and it
works here for the same reason it worked on the PDP-11.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libm.a
.br
lib/libm/sin.c, tan.c, asin.c, atan.c
